{"cluster_id": "C-0543", "topic": "CAS correctness", "target": "architecture/correctness", "member_ids": ["AUDIT-M2-215"], "canonical_claim": "The CA transient-classifier audit flags two ErrorCode-consistency mistags on the Mount plane: `CasServerRoot.cpp:261`'s 100-attempt CAS loop is coded `CORRUPTED_DATA` while sibling sites use `ABORTED`, and `:241` bundles `ContainerAbsent`/`AccessDenied`/`Indeterminate` together as corruption; both are contained but inconsistent.", "issue_ids": [], "sources": ["docs/superpowers/reports/2026-07-29-ca-transient-classifier-audit.md#L86-L88"]}
{"cluster_id": "C-0544", "topic": "CAS correctness", "target": "architecture/correctness", "member_ids": ["AUDIT-M2-231"], "canonical_claim": "D3 (mostly duplicated with meaningful differences): three separate conditional-write retry regimes exist for the same class of operation \u2014 `CasRequestController` (ref lane: single-attempt client, budget, fence-gated, resolve-before-reissue), plain-object `casPutObject`/`casRemoveObject` loops (`MAX_CAS_ATTEMPTS=100`, default retry policy), and `CasBuild`'s local loops (`max_attempts=8`) \u2014 and only the ref lane implements the lease-deadline model; the plain-object gap is a real hazard.", "issue_ids": ["D3"], "sources": ["docs/superpowers/reports/2026-07-12-cas-archaeology/00-REPORT.md#L524"]}
{"cluster_id": "C-0545", "topic": "CAS correctness", "target": "architecture/correctness", "member_ids": ["AUDIT-M2-237"], "canonical_claim": "D9: `ObjectStorageBackend` implements overlapping token-policy logic (native ETag vs emulated vs Generation) independently across `head`/`list`/`casPut`/`deleteExact`/`supportsListTokens`, corroborated by a past real incident (the `EmulatedSingleProcess` list-vs-head token mismatch); this is called out as the highest-value consolidation target in the report.", "issue_ids": ["D9"], "sources": ["docs/superpowers/reports/2026-07-12-cas-archaeology/00-REPORT.md#L530"]}
{"cluster_id": "C-0546", "topic": "CAS correctness", "target": "architecture/correctness", "member_ids": ["AUDIT-M2-242"], "canonical_claim": "Risk register #8 (Medium, confirmed): the destructive `GC REBUILD` verb (which overwrites `gc/state` and, when no disk is given, loops over ALL CA disks) shares one access right with the benign per-round `GC` verb (`AccessType.h:351`; `InterpreterSystemQuery.cpp:1004,1010`), so an operator cannot grant \"run a round\" without also granting disaster-recovery rebuild, and a broad accidental `SYSTEM ... GC REBUILD` rebuilds every pool. Suggested action: a dedicated access right; consider requiring an explicit disk for rebuild.", "issue_ids": [], "sources": ["docs/superpowers/reports/2026-07-12-cas-archaeology/00-REPORT.md#L549"]}
{"cluster_id": "C-0547", "topic": "CAS correctness", "target": "architecture/correctness", "member_ids": ["AUDIT-M2-247"], "canonical_claim": "Risk register #13 (Med-Low, confirmed): `WriteSettings.h:36-63` adds six S3 knobs including `shared_ptr<const S3::Client> s3_client_override` to a hot, widely-included, copied-per-write struct, whose `operator==` compares the pointer \u2014 this creates refcount traffic on hot write paths, surprising equality semantics, and a wide recompile blast radius. Suggested action: measure; consider a narrower CA-path-only struct.", "issue_ids": [], "sources": ["docs/superpowers/reports/2026-07-12-cas-archaeology/00-REPORT.md#L554"]}
{"cluster_id": "C-0548", "topic": "CAS correctness", "target": "architecture/correctness", "member_ids": ["AUDIT-M2-313"], "canonical_claim": "The claim that a rewritten CAS `fsck` oracle is \"seal-aware\" is overstated: current `fsck` discovers snapshots and logs solely via untrusted `LIST`, silently skips namespaces when expected evidence is unavailable, and has no `unchecked` term for a missing or pointer/seal-mismatched authority, so it could be implemented by special-casing synthetic snapshot IDs while still accepting a listed ghost as clean.", "issue_ids": [], "sources": ["docs/superpowers/reports/2026-07-28-ref-rework-reviews/codex_r4_findings.md#L77-L83"]}
{"cluster_id": "C-0549", "topic": "CAS correctness", "target": "architecture/correctness", "member_ids": ["AUDIT-M2-369"], "canonical_claim": "An explicit `<use_fake_transaction>true</use_fake_transaction>` on a `content_addressed` disk is silently honored, causing `createTransaction()` to return a per-file autocommit `FakeDiskTransaction` with no commit point and breaking atomic manifest/ref publish; proposed fix is to throw `BAD_ARGUMENTS` at registration when the key is explicitly true on a CAS disk.", "issue_ids": [], "sources": ["docs/superpowers/reports/2026-07-20-umbrella-review-cas-vs-antalya-26.6.md#L39-L43"]}
{"cluster_id": "C-0550", "topic": "CAS correctness", "target": "architecture/correctness", "member_ids": ["B001-027"], "canonical_claim": "A live ref must resolve through present keys; an absent key is a surfaced storage exception rather than a silent empty read (INV-NO-DANGLE).", "issue_ids": [], "sources": ["docs/superpowers/cas/01-architecture.md#L533-L549"]}
{"cluster_id": "C-0551", "topic": "CAS correctness", "target": "architecture/correctness", "member_ids": ["B001-040", "B012-082"], "canonical_claim": "CasRequestController::putIfAbsentControlled gates every attempt by the fence and remaining deadline, resolves uncertain writes by exact GET, and treats differing existing bytes as CORRUPTED_DATA.; After an uncertain immutable conditional create, identical deterministic bytes at the exact key prove commit, a different valid object is a conflict, absence permits another attempt only after budget checks, and read errors or unprovable identity remain uncertain.", "issue_ids": [], "sources": ["docs/superpowers/reports/2026-07-12-cas-archaeology/02-store-core.md#L189-L195", "docs/superpowers/specs/2026-07-12-cas-s3-timeout-retry-control-rfc.md#resolve-before-reissuing"]}
{"cluster_id": "C-0552", "topic": "CAS correctness", "target": "architecture/correctness", "member_ids": ["B001-075"], "canonical_claim": "Build::promote validates the manifest, checks the precommit owner, revalidates required blob leaves, and publishes the precommit-to-committed binding only after those checks.", "issue_ids": [], "sources": ["docs/superpowers/cas/03-writer-protocol.md#L313-L347"]}
{"cluster_id": "C-0553", "topic": "CAS correctness", "target": "architecture/correctness", "member_ids": ["B002-002"], "canonical_claim": "GC must not delete an object while any session pin or committed reference names it, preserving `INV_NO_LOSS` and `INV_NO_DANGLE`.", "issue_ids": [], "sources": ["docs/superpowers/cas/04-gc-protocol.md#overview"]}
{"cluster_id": "C-0554", "topic": "CAS correctness", "target": "architecture/correctness", "member_ids": ["B002-020", "B011-025", "B017-041", "B038-017"], "canonical_claim": "Deterministic GC artifacts such as in-degree runs and fold seals must be byte-identical on collision, otherwise GC fails with `CORRUPTED_DATA`. Additionally, Run and seal adoption continue to use `putDeterministicArtifact`: `putIfAbsent` collisions must be byte-equal or fail with `CORRUPTED_DATA`, and `gc/state` adopts the generation and attempt through the seal.; `cas_run` is `RecordStream` with `Strict` keys and `PinnedRaw` storage, and its byte-deterministic output is required for `putDeterministicArtifact` adoption.; Deterministic formats such as `cas_fold_seal` and `cas_run` use strict keys and pinned raw encoding; a `putDeterministicArtifact` conflict is resolved by re-encoding at the version of the existing object.", "issue_ids": [], "sources": ["docs/superpowers/cas/04-gc-protocol.md#attempt-scoped-design", "docs/superpowers/plans/2026-07-15-cas-codecs-v3-phase5-runs.md#global-constraints", "docs/superpowers/specs/2026-07-10-cas-retired-in-snapshot-design.md#adoption", "src/Disks/DiskObjectStorage/MetadataStorages/ContentAddressed/Formats/README.md#evolution-rules"]}
{"cluster_id": "C-0555", "topic": "CAS correctness", "target": "architecture/correctness", "member_ids": ["B002-022", "B010-086"], "canonical_claim": "A crash before the round CAS leaves the prior generation authoritative; the next pass reruns from a fresh attempt and previously issued exact-token deletes are idempotent through `NotFound` or `TokenMismatch`. Additionally, The failure table requires a lost GC generation CAS to leave the attempt unadopted and delete no logs, while a stop after a successful CAS treats the new fold as authoritative and repeats covered-log deletion later.", "issue_ids": [], "sources": ["docs/superpowers/cas/04-gc-protocol.md#deletes-publish-cas", "docs/superpowers/specs/2026-07-10-cas-ref-snapshot-log-design.md#failure-handling"]}
{"cluster_id": "C-0556", "topic": "CAS correctness", "target": "architecture/correctness", "member_ids": ["B002-034", "B005-029"], "canonical_claim": "A retired entry whose in-degree recovers above zero is spared even when it is already `delete_pending`. Additionally, A pending delete is terminal: a pending entry with recovered in-degree is spared and logged loudly, while re-executing a pending delete is safe at any leader staleness; a failed CAS cannot publish a zombie leader's fresh decisions.", "issue_ids": [], "sources": ["docs/superpowers/cas/04-gc-protocol.md#three-cursor-merge", "docs/superpowers/specs/2026-07-02-cas-gc-ack-floor-fence-redesign.md#gc-round-protocol-one-pass"]}
{"cluster_id": "C-0557", "topic": "CAS correctness", "target": "architecture/correctness", "member_ids": ["B002-038"], "canonical_claim": "If an exact delete reports `created_delete_marker = true`, GC throws `LOGICAL_ERROR` because versioning tombstones can violate `INV_NO_RETURN`.", "issue_ids": [], "sources": ["docs/superpowers/cas/04-gc-protocol.md#deletes-publish-cas"]}
{"cluster_id": "C-0558", "topic": "CAS correctness", "target": "architecture/correctness", "member_ids": ["B002-052", "B004-018"], "canonical_claim": "GC ordering is biased toward over-counting: crashes may delay reclamation, but must never make GC under-count a live reference. Additionally, CAS failure handling is conservative: lost folds, stale snapshots, duplicated records, and crashed leaders can only postpone reclamation because in-degree is an idempotent set of source edges rather than a counter.", "issue_ids": [], "sources": ["docs/superpowers/cas/04-gc-protocol.md#safety-invariants", "docs/superpowers/cas/11-walkthrough.md#safety-invariants"]}
{"cluster_id": "C-0559", "topic": "CAS correctness", "target": "architecture/correctness", "member_ids": ["B002-055"], "canonical_claim": "A point-in-time `HEAD -> 404` is not durable absence; unreadable bodies clamp the fold and any clamped pass suppresses destructive decisions.", "issue_ids": [], "sources": ["docs/superpowers/cas/04-gc-protocol.md#absent-at-head"]}
{"cluster_id": "C-0560", "topic": "CAS correctness", "target": "architecture/correctness", "member_ids": ["B002-092"], "canonical_claim": "Per-hash metadata is an advisory freshness point-read, not the blob lifetime linearization point; exactness remains with the in-body incarnation tag and exact-token body delete.", "issue_ids": [], "sources": ["docs/superpowers/cas/05-formats-and-backend.md#per-hash-meta"]}
{"cluster_id": "C-0561", "topic": "CAS correctness", "target": "architecture/correctness", "member_ids": ["B002-135"], "canonical_claim": "CA tests write objects through the same production codecs used by readers, so the fixtures exercise real format round trips rather than white-box state pokes.", "issue_ids": [], "sources": ["docs/superpowers/reports/2026-07-12-cas-archaeology/06-tests.md#2-test-infrastructure-cas-test-helpers-h-patterns"]}
{"cluster_id": "C-0562", "topic": "CAS correctness", "target": "architecture/correctness", "member_ids": ["B002-138"], "canonical_claim": "The test suite scripts concurrency hazards through backend subclasses and does not use sleeps to reproduce races; only `gtest_cas_part_folder_access.cpp` and `gtest_cas_ref_writer.cpp` use real `std::thread`.", "issue_ids": [], "sources": ["docs/superpowers/reports/2026-07-12-cas-archaeology/06-tests.md#2-test-infrastructure-cas-test-helpers-h-patterns"]}
{"cluster_id": "C-0563", "topic": "CAS correctness", "target": "architecture/correctness", "member_ids": ["B002-141"], "canonical_claim": "CA tests are single-process, single-threaded, and clock-free for GC scenarios; genuine concurrency correctness rests on backend primitives, TLA+ models, and integration or soak tests rather than gtests alone.", "issue_ids": [], "sources": ["docs/superpowers/reports/2026-07-12-cas-archaeology/06-tests.md#4-oracle-patterns-strongest-weakest"]}
{"cluster_id": "C-0564", "topic": "CAS correctness", "target": "architecture/correctness", "member_ids": ["B002-158"], "canonical_claim": "`CaIncarnationCore.tla` proves `INV_NO_DANGLE`, `INV_NO_LOSS`, `INV_NO_RETURN`, and `INV_JOURNAL_COVERAGE` across adversarial writers, GC leaders, split-brain, resurrection, overwrite, and trimming scenarios.", "issue_ids": [], "sources": ["docs/superpowers/cas/06-tla-models.md#caincarnationcore"]}
{"cluster_id": "C-0565", "topic": "CAS correctness", "target": "architecture/correctness", "member_ids": ["B002-160", "B035-067"], "canonical_claim": "A writer publish gate must consult durable `deadTok[h]` as well as in-flight retired state; consulting only retired state can admit a physically dead token after deletion.; The CaIncarnationCore publish gate must consult durable deleted-token history `deadTok[h]`, not only the in-flight `retired` set.", "issue_ids": [], "sources": ["docs/superpowers/cas/06-tla-models.md#caincarnationcore", "docs/superpowers/cas/CONSOLIDATION-COVERAGE.md#blocking-gaps"]}
